SECTION VII.
Simulation Output

Statistical output is very important because it gives information that cannot be fully observed by just looking at graphical representations of a traffic simulation. That is why most of the simulation packages include some output files or data. The following includes the data and files found in each simulation package.

    SUMO

    Route Detectors: detector for sampling route distributions

        Edge lane traffic: edge or lane-based network performance measures

        Trip information: aggregated information about each vehicle's journey

        Vehicle routes information: information about each vehicle's routes over simulation run

        Simulation state statistics: information about the current state of the simulation

    Aimsun

        Statistical simulation results: Network, Section, Node

        Output database definition

        Calculation of traffic statistics

        Experiment output

        Map output

    Vissim

        Scenario Management: Comparing scenario results conveniently

        Enhanced evaluation possibilities: Level of Service results for nodes

        Improved handling of the matrices through matrix editor

        Visualisation highlights

        Vehicle record

        Vehicle travel times (raw data)

        Pedestrian record

        Nodes (raw data)

        Signal changes

SECTION VIII.
Ability to Simulate Very Large Traffic Networks

Traffic networks are simulated to plan specific changes and observe its effects to a network. It may be a single road leading to a city or a very complex downtown area of a large city. That is why it is important for a simulation package to have an option of simulating very large traffic networks containing hundreds of roads and thousands of vehicles running.

SUMO, Aimsun, and Vissim has the ability to cope with large traffic networks. But as expected the larger the network the slower the frame rate was during simulation.
